## Which one should you actually buy?

- **You care most about how the shot tastes:** Flair Neo, paired with a proper espresso-capable grinder. Nothing else here matches it on quality per dollar.
- **You want the cheapest plug-in starter:** De'Longhi Stilosa. Accept the pressurized-basket ceiling as the price of $100 convenience.
- **You want push-button lattes for a household:** De'Longhi Dedica EC685 — the small boiler is the tradeoff for the speed and ease.
- **You travel or have no counter space:** Wacaco Nanopresso, and keep a kettle nearby.

## The grinder truth nobody selling machines wants you to hear

Here's the part that decides everything: for espresso, **your grinder matters more than your machine.** Espresso needs a fine, precise, adjustable grind, and a blade grinder or a coarse-only burr simply cannot produce it — you'll get channeling, gushers, and sour shots no matter which machine above you buy. This is doubly true for the non-pressurized Flair Neo, which has no forgiving valve to hide behind.

Budget accordingly: if you're buying a Flair, plan to spend on an espresso-capable grinder too. Several manual grinders in our [budget grinder guide](best-budget-coffee-grinders-under-60.html) reach espresso fineness, though a dedicated espresso grinder does it better. And a [0.1g scale from our pour-over kit](complete-pour-over-coffee-setup-under-50.html) is just as essential here — espresso is a ratio game (roughly 1:2, coffee to liquid out), and dosing by eye wrecks consistency.

If all of this sounds like more project than you signed up for, that's a completely reasonable conclusion — and it's worth reading our [AeroPress vs French press comparison](aeropress-vs-french-press-budget-brewer.html) before committing. Neither makes true espresso, but the AeroPress makes a concentrated, milk-friendly cup with a fraction of the fuss and cost.

## FAQ

**Can any machine under $200 make "real" espresso?**
Yes — the Flair Neo makes genuinely real, non-pressurized espresso, and the Nanopresso makes a legitimate (if tiny) pressurized shot. The two De'Longhi machines make real espresso through pressurized baskets, which is real espresso with training wheels: good, but with a quality ceiling.

**What's a pressurized basket, and why does it matter?**
It's a portafilter basket with a single small hole and a valve that artificially builds back-pressure, so you get crema even with a poor grind. It makes beginners' shots forgiving, but it caps how good your espresso can get. Non-pressurized baskets reward good technique and a good grinder with a noticeably better cup.

**Do I need a separate grinder, or can I use pre-ground espresso?**
Pre-ground espresso works acceptably in pressurized baskets (Stilosa, Dedica) and is the easy route. For the Flair Neo's non-pressurized basket, fresh fine grinding isn't optional — pre-ground goes stale fast and rarely lands at the right fineness. Budget for a grinder if you go manual.

**Is a $200 machine enough for good lattes?**
For milk drinks, the Dedica's steam wand is the best here and makes solid cappuccinos with practice. The Stilosa can steam milk but slowly and weakly. The Flair and Nanopresso don't steam milk at all — you'd froth separately with a cheap handheld wand.

**Should I just save up for a Gaggia Classic instead?**
If espresso is a long-term hobby and you can stretch to ~$450, the Gaggia Classic Pro is a genuine step up (non-pressurized capable, real steam, endlessly repairable). But that's a different budget. Everything above earns its place strictly under $200.
